🚀 URL Shortener — Final Interview Notes (Perfect to Speak)
1. Problem Statement

“A URL shortener converts long URLs into short, unique codes and redirects users to the original URL with very low latency.”

2. Functional Requirements

“Users should be able to shorten URLs, get redirects instantly, optionally set custom aliases, expiry times, and track basic click counts.”

3. Non-Functional Requirements

“The system must be highly available, low-latency, scalable, and guarantee unique short codes.”

4. Core APIs

“I’ll provide two main APIs:
• POST /shorten to create short URLs
• GET /{code} to redirect to the long URL.”

5. High-Level Architecture

“Requests flow through a Load Balancer to stateless App Servers, which use Redis Cache for fast lookups and a NoSQL Database for persistent storage.”

(Speak as architecture) →
Client → Load Balancer → App Servers → Redis → NoSQL DB

6. Short Code Generation

“I generate short codes by storing the URL, taking the auto-increment ID, and converting it into a Base62 string (a–z, A–Z, 0–9) for compact, unique keys.”

7. Database Choice

“I use a NoSQL key-value store like DynamoDB or Cassandra since our lookups are simple and read-heavy. Key is the short code, value is the long URL.”

8. Caching (Critical Section)

“Most traffic is redirects, so Redis caches shortCode → longURL to return results in microseconds and reduce database load.”

9. Load Balancing

“A load balancer evenly distributes requests across multiple app servers, ensuring both scalability and high availability.”

10. Scalability

“The system scales horizontally because app servers are stateless. The database can be sharded by short code range for large-scale traffic.”

11. Collision Handling

“Auto-increment ID + Base62 ensures uniqueness, so collisions naturally never happen.”

12. Optional Enhancements

“We can add link expiration, custom aliases, QR codes, analytics, and user dashboards as optional features.”

⭐ FINAL 25-SECOND INTERVIEW SUMMARY (Say This When Asked “Explain URL Shortener”)

“I’d design a URL shortener with stateless app servers behind a load balancer.
When a user submits a URL, I store it in a NoSQL DB, take the auto-generated ID, and encode it using Base62 to get a short, unique code.
Redirects are extremely read-heavy, so I cache shortCode-to-longURL mappings in Redis for microsecond responses.
The system scales horizontally, avoids collisions, and supports optional features like custom aliases and expiration.”
